GeoNode #13366 (Inteligeo issue inteligeo-deploy#759): registering a remote service
ends in HTTP 500, "TypeError: Object of type WmsServiceHandler is not JSON
serializable", because geonode/services/views.py stores the service handler
object in the Django session and the Inteligeo settings serialize sessions as
JSON (SESSION_SERIALIZER = JSONSerializer, inteligeo-geonode commit bb8ea5a,
2026-02-06, chosen so that a leaked SECRET_KEY cannot become code execution
through a pickled session). Not a CVE; a functional fix of GeoNode 4.3.1 in the
setting GeoNode 5 made the default.

Target: GeoNode 4.3.1 as installed by this image (site-packages/geonode). Applied
with `patch -p1 --fuzz=0` from site-packages (paths are geonode/...).

Origin: GeoNode PR #13384 "[Fixes #13366] Replace service handler sessions
serialization with response caches", commit
e132e0fc5d46e0184528c9380955b7e078529e56 (master, 2025-08-26), completed by
commit 75a64462c1 (PR #13397, upgrade to Django 5.2, 2025-10-01), which switched
GeoNode itself to the JSONSerializer (Django 5 removed PickleSerializer) and
commented out the last session read. That is the state released in 5.0.3:
services/views.py lines 78-81 (register_service), 108-111
(_get_service_handler) and 200-208 (harvest_resources). The origin issue states
the reason: a session must hold data, not an arbitrary object.

Transposed: the removal of the session storage. In 4.3.1 the handler was
written at lines 74 (register_service) and 97 (_get_service_handler), read at
line 190 (harvest_resources, falling back to _get_service_handler on KeyError)
and popped at line 288 (service_detail). After the patch every view rebuilds
the handler from the Service row (service_url, type, id), exactly as
harvest_single_resource (line 206) and rescan_service (line 224) already did in
4.3.1, so no reader depends on the session any more. The pop in service_detail
and its comment are removed too, because nothing writes the key; the origin
kept that dead code (5.0.3 line 264).

Not transposed: the per-process cache of handlers the origin added in
serviceprocessors/__init__.py (`caches["services"]`, new settings
CACHES["services"] and SERVICE_CACHE_EXPIRATION_TIME). It needs a settings
change in every project that imports geonode.settings and it is evaluated at
import time, so a project without the alias fails to import geonode.services;
and it buys little here: in 4.3.1 WmsServiceHandler.parsed_service (wms.py
lines 93-97) re-reads the capabilities on every access and never fills
_parsed_service, so the pickled handler only carried url, name, indexing_method
and geonode_service_id. What the session saved was the one GetCapabilities
request made by WmsServiceHandler.__init__ (line 62, _offers_geonode_projection);
the harvest page and its POST now make that request once each (measured 1.2-1.5 s
against a public WMS). Left to the GeoNode 5 migration.

Why a patch here and not a 14th override in inteligeo-geonode: the file has no
Inteligeo logic, the defect is GeoNode's under the session serializer that
GeoNode 5 itself adopted, and an override would copy 290 lines to carry a
26-line deletion, hiding the difference; the patch is the difference.

Files touched: geonode/services/views.py. Not among the 13 library files
inteligeo-geonode copies over the installed packages (its Dockerfile, lines
48-78: eleven of GeoNode, one of pycsw, one of the importer).

Test run (2026-09-21): applied on a clean copy of the installed package with
`patch -p1 --fuzz=0 --no-backup-if-mismatch`: 1 file, 4 hunks, 0 FAILED, 0
fuzz; `python -m py_compile` clean; the two SSRF patches applied before it in
the same run, 0 FAILED. Behaviour in the Inteligeo pod (instancia_r, patched
file copied into the running container, uwsgi touch-reload), with the public
WMS https://ows.terrestris.de/osm/service: unpatched, POST /services/register/
-> 500 and GET /services/<id>/harvest -> 500, both with the TypeError above
raised from django/contrib/sessions/middleware.py process_response
(geonode.log); patched, register -> 302 to /services/31/harvest, the harvest
page -> 200 listing the 11 layers of the WMS, POST with one layer -> 302 and a
harvesting session "finished-all-ok" that created the remote dataset
"OSM-WMS", service detail -> 200, rescan -> 302 with a refresh session
"finished-all-ok" (11/11), harvest of a single layer by URL -> 302 and a second
remote dataset; remove -> 302 leaving 0 services, 0 harvesters, 0 harvestable
resources, 0 remote datasets and no WMS store in GeoServer. No new occurrence
of the TypeError in geonode.log after the patch.

